<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net11.0</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>Arronix.Architecture.Tests.MovieCatalogerFixture</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Arronix.Architecture.Tests.MovieCatalogerFixture</AssemblyName>
    <Version>0.1.0</Version>
    <Description>Stands in for a separately shipped movie provider package. It compiles ICataloger&lt;Movie&gt; and ICurator&lt;Movie&gt; against the universal contracts and the movies media domain only, so the reference set it needs is a checkable fact rather than a claim.</Description>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
       The whole point of the fixture is what is missing here. A vendor package pairs with a media kind by
       closing a generic over that kind's item type, and it must be able to do so without taking on the
       media extension's parser, module, definition or generated reader delegates - which have a different
       isolation, update and unload lifetime. Adding Arronix.Plugin.Movies to this list would still
       compile; the architecture tests fail instead, because compiling is exactly what makes the mistake
       invisible.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Arronix.Abstractions\Arronix.Abstractions.csproj" />
    <!--
         Compilation only. The movies media domain is published by the movies package this one requires,
         and a private copy here would be a second CLR identity for the exact type the pairing is about.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Arronix.Media.Movies\Arronix.Media.Movies.csproj" Private="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="plugin.json" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
